    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.resistance_distance_matrix - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.resistance_distance_matrix.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.resistance_distance_matrix()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate the resistance distance matrix for all node pairs.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.resistance_distance_matrix()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate the resistance distance matrix for all node pairs.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.resistance_distance_matrix()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Calculate the resistance distance matrix for all node pairs.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCalculate the resistance distance matrix for all node pairs.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L85167-L85269){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.resistance_distance_matrix()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Resistance distance matrix](../assets/graphs/resistance-distance-matrix.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A five-node cycle A–C–B–E–D. The table gives the resistance distance for every pair: 0 on the diagonal, 0.8 for adjacent nodes, and 1.2 for nodes two steps apart; A and B are outlined in the accent color and their 1.2 cells are highlighted.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns a dictionary mapping (source_id, target_id) tuples to their resistance distances.\nThe matrix is symmetric with zeros on the diagonal.\n\nResistance distance treats each edge as a 1-ohm resistor and measures the effective\nresistance between two nodes, so it reflects every route between them rather than just\nthe shortest one (see `resistance_distance()` for details). Computing all pairs at\nonce is much cheaper than calling `resistance_distance()` repeatedly, because the\nexpensive matrix step is shared. Use the full matrix when you need:\n\n- a pairwise distance matrix for clustering, embedding, or visualization\n- the most and least robustly connected node pairs in a network\n- inputs for other measures built from resistance, such as the Kirchhoff index\n  (also available directly via `kirchhoff_index()`)\n\nEdge weights are ignored (every edge is a 1-ohm resistor), parallel edges act as\nresistors in parallel, and self-loops are ignored. Pairs in different connected\ncomponents get `float(\"inf\")`, since no current can flow between them; each component\nis solved on its own. The result has `n * n` entries, so memory grows quadratically\nwith the number of nodes.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[tuple[int, int], float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Dictionary mapping node ID pairs to resistance distances. Both orientations of every\n    pair are present (`(u, v)` and `(v, u)` hold the same value), as are the diagonal\n    entries `(u, u)`, which are `0.0`. Pairs in different connected components hold\n    `float(\"inf\")`. The keys run through `get_node_ids()` order, row by row. An empty\n    graph gives an empty dictionary.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is directed.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nBuilds the full symmetric matrix of pairwise effective resistances in a single\n$O(n^3)$ computation of the Laplacian pseudoinverse $L^+$, from which every entry\nis recovered as $\\Omega(u, v) = L^+_{uu} + L^+_{vv} - 2 L^+_{uv}$. That shared-cost\nstructure makes the matrix substantially cheaper than $\\binom{n}{2}$ individual\n`resistance_distance()` calls whenever more than a handful of pairs are needed.\nThe returned dictionary contains both orientations for every ordered pair for\nlookup convenience; entries on the diagonal are $0$.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~resistance_distance`) \n\n:   The resistance between one pair.\n\n[](`~kirchhoff_index`) \n\n:   The sum of the matrix over all pairs.\n\n[](`~current_flow_closeness_centrality`) \n\n:   Rank nodes by their row sums.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe dictionary holds both orders of each pair. In a triangle with a tail, the corners\nare joined by two routes, and the tail by one:\n\n::: {#6bb7756f .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")]\n)\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n\n{\n    (label(u), label(v)): round(r, 3)\n    for (u, v), r in graph.resistance_distance_matrix().items()\n    if label(u) < label(v)\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{('C', 'D'): 1.0,\n ('A', 'C'): 0.667,\n ('A', 'D'): 1.667,\n ('A', 'B'): 0.667,\n ('B', 'C'): 0.667,\n ('B', 'D'): 1.667}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Best connected, counting every route\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network, each station placed at its `pos` on the map.\nAdding up a station's resistance to all the others measures how well it's connected by\nevery route, not only the shortest. Market, where the Red and Ring lines meet, comes out\nahead of Central, and the Airport, at the end of its own line, comes last:\n\n::: {#583dd4e4 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\ntotal = {n: 0.0 for n in metro.get_node_ids()}\nfor (u, v), r in metro.resistance_distance_matrix().items():\n    total[u] += r\n\nranked = sorted(total, key=total.get)\n[(metro.get_label_from_id(n), round(total[n], 2)) for n in ranked[:3]]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[('Market', 26.38), ('Central', 26.67), ('Riverside', 27.04)]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#0146d3b7 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro.set_node_attrs(\"resistance\", total).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"resistance\", color_scale=\"sequential\",\n    label_by=\"label\",\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-17eebb88:#83b3ec;--ect-715207f9:#82b2eb;--ect-a9ec32de:#234c82;--ect-4eae359b:#74a3dc;--ect-3b99d646:#709fd8;--ect-d37859ca:#6f9ed6;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-d77ae92f:#5d8ac2;--ect-ce3c29cd:#5884bc;--ect-5b3fd3f2:#85b5ee;--ect-d06136e8:#5f8cc5;--ect-05c74c51:#6492ca;--ect-71053e5c:#72a0d9;--ect-34ac2e54:#6896ce;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-61ed3c93:#6390c9;--ect-de17e7ab:#426da5;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-e6b